// QT question bank. Written from the LMS Module 1-5 Ebooks and Study Guides only.
// Row: [module, question, options, correctIndex (0-based), reason, source, wrong-option notes]
window.QBANK = {
  subject: "Quantitative Techniques", code: "QT",
  modules: {"1":"Statistics — An Introduction","2":"Classification of Data","3":"Tabulation","4":"Presentation & Collection of Data","5":"Measures of Central Tendency"},
  rows: [
[1,"In its plural sense, statistics refers to:",["A scientific method for data analysis","A set of numerical figures or data","The branch of mathematics dealing with probability","A method for collecting qualitative information"],1,"In plural sense, statistics = numerical facts/data (e.g., sales figures over 10 years).","Ebook","Other options: Singular sense = scientific method for collection, classification, analysis, and interpretation · Do not confuse the two senses — MCQs often flip them · Qualitative data is NOT statistics in the technical sense"],
[1,"In its singular sense, statistics is best described as:",["A set of numerical data collected by the government","A method of presenting data in tables and charts","A scientific method for collection, classification, analysis, and interpretation of numerical data","Numerical facts affected by a single cause"],2,"Singular sense = statistics as a scientific method involving all four stages: collection, classification, analysis, interpretation.","Ebook","Other options: Plural sense = just the numerical data themselves · Collection alone is not the full singular-sense definition · Yule & Kendall's definition covers the plural sense"],
[1,"Which author defined statistics as 'Numerical facts placed in relation to each other'?",["Yule & Kendall","Webster","A.L. Bowley","R.A. Fisher"],2,"A.L. Bowley defined statistics as 'Numerical facts placed in relation to each other.'","Ebook","Other options: Yule & Kendall: 'Quantitative data affected by multiplicity of causes' · Webster: 'Classified facts respecting conditions of people, stated in numbers' · R.A. Fisher wrote 'Statistical Methods for Research Workers' (1925) — not a definitional author"],
[1,"Yule & Kendall defined statistics as 'quantitative data affected by ___':",["A single dominant factor","Multiplicity of causes","Government classification","Random sampling only"],1,"Yule & Kendall: 'Quantitative data affected by multiplicity of causes' — reflecting that real-world data is influenced by many simultaneous factors.","Ebook","Other options: A.L. Bowley's definition focuses on 'facts placed in relation to each other' · Webster's definition emphasises 'conditions of people, stated in numbers' · Multiplicity of causes is also a characteristic of statistics — double-check context"],
[1,"R.A. Fisher's book 'Statistical Methods for Research Workers' was published in:",["1905","1935","1925","1945"],2,"R.A. Fisher published 'Statistical Methods for Research Workers' in 1925, marking the beginning of modern statistics.","Ebook","Other options: 1925 — not 1905, 1935, or 1945 · Fisher is associated with modern statistics, not classical statistics · This date is a direct MCQ target"],
[1,"Which of the following is NOT a characteristic of statistics?",["Aggregate of facts","Affected by multiplicity of factors","Based on individual observations only","Capable of being placed in relation to each other"],2,"Statistics deals with aggregates/groups, NOT individual observations. A single figure is not statistics.","Ebook","Other options: Statistics must be an aggregate — never based on a single observation · All 6 characteristics are frequent MCQ targets · Numerical figures, aggregate, multiplicity, systematic collection, predetermined purpose, relatable — these are the 6"],
[1,"The characteristic 'enumerated or estimated with reasonable accuracy' means:",["Statistics must always be exact, not estimated","Data must be collected by counting or measurement with acceptable precision","Statistical figures must be verified by an expert before use","Data can be collected haphazardly"],1,"This characteristic means data is collected by counting or measurement — reasonable accuracy is acceptable, but wild estimates are not.","Ebook","Other options: Perfect accuracy is not required — 'reasonable' accuracy is the standard · Haphazard collection leads to wrong conclusions — violates another characteristic · This is different from 'systematic collection' which is a separate characteristic"],
[1,"Which branch of theoretical statistics makes generalisations about a population from a sample?",["Descriptive Statistics","Applied Statistics","Inferential Statistics","Inductive Statistics"],3,"Inductive statistics makes generalisations about a population based on sample data and includes forecasting.","Ebook","Other options: Descriptive statistics = collection, classification, tabulation, diagrams, averages · Inferential statistics = tests hypotheses about population characteristics · Inductive vs Inferential is a common MCQ trap — know the distinction"],
[1,"Descriptive statistics includes all of the following EXCEPT:",["Collection and classification of data","Diagrams and averages","Testing hypotheses about population","Correlation and dispersion"],2,"Testing hypotheses is inferential statistics. Descriptive statistics covers collection, classification, tabulation, diagrams, averages, dispersion, and correlation.","Ebook","Other options: Hypothesis testing = Inferential, not Descriptive · Forecasting = Inductive statistics · Applied statistics uses all three in practical settings"],
[1,"Statistics is called both a science and an art because:",["It uses both numbers and diagrams","Its systematic application makes it a science; successful use requires skill, making it an art","Governments and businesses both use it","It combines descriptive and inferential methods"],1,"Science = systematic, general application; Art = successful use depends on the statistician's skill and experience.","Ebook","Other options: Science aspect: systematic principles, general applicability · Art aspect: skill and experience of the statistician determine quality · This is a theory question — often appears in descriptive/explanation format"],
[1,"In which area of business is statistics used for setting UCL and LCL?",["Accounts and auditing","Production planning","Quality control","Insurance"],2,"Quality control uses statistical control charts with Upper Control Limit (UCL) and Lower Control Limit (LCL) to monitor product consistency.","Ebook","Other options: UCL/LCL are quality control terms, not accounting terms · Insurance uses mortality tables and cash flow estimation · Inventory control uses optimal stock level analysis"],
[1,"Which function of statistics involves studying the relationship between two variables?",["Simplifying complex data","Policy formulation","Studying relationships between phenomena (correlation)","Presenting facts in numerical figures"],2,"Studying relationships between phenomena through correlation is one of the 9 functions of statistics.","Ebook","Other options: Correlation studies relationships; regression is used for prediction/forecasting · Policy formulation is a separate function of statistics · Simplifying complex data is also a function but not the relationship-study function"],
[1,"Which limitation states that statistics 'deals with groups, not individuals'?",["Results are only approximately true","Statistics deals with numerical facts only","Results are true only on average; individual exceptions are ignored","Statistics can be misused"],2,"This limitation means statistical results (e.g., average wage) do not apply to every individual — a worker may earn much more or less than the average.","Ebook","Other options: This is limitations #2 and #3 combined in practice · Misuse of statistics is limitation #6 · Qualitative traits cannot be measured = limitation #1"],
[1,"Yule & Kendall said statistics is 'the most dangerous tool in the hands of ___':",["A politician","An inexpert","A biased researcher","A non-mathematician"],1,"Yule & Kendall cautioned that statistics must be used by experts — it is the most dangerous tool in the hands of an inexpert.","Ebook","Other options: This is limitation #7: must be used by experts only · Misuse can arise from biased collection OR from lack of expertise · The quote is from Yule & Kendall, not Bowley or Fisher"],
[1,"Statistics can be applied in national income accounting to estimate:",["Stock prices and index values","GDP, savings, and investment","Mortality rates only","Quality control limits"],1,"National income accounting uses statistics to estimate GDP, savings, and investment — key macroeconomic indicators.","Ebook","Other options: Stock prices = business/financial application, not national income accounting · Mortality rates = insurance application · Quality control = manufacturing/business application"],
[1,"Which of the following is NOT one of the 9 functions of statistics?",["Decision making under uncertainty","Forecasting via time series and regression","Testing hypotheses","Replacing all qualitative assessments with numbers"],3,"Replacing qualitative assessments is not a function of statistics — in fact, statistics cannot handle purely qualitative data (limitation #1).","Ebook","Other options: The 9 functions include: present facts, simplify data, study relationships, compare, enlarge experience, policy, forecast, test hypotheses, decision under uncertainty · Statistics cannot quantify beauty, honesty, etc. — that is a limitation · Decision under uncertainty IS a function"],
[1,"The use of statistical sampling techniques in accounts writing is an example of statistics applied to:",["Quality control","Insurance","Accounts and auditing","National income accounting"],2,"Sampling techniques in auditing allow auditors to check a sample of transactions to draw conclusions about the full ledger.","Ebook","Other options: Quality control uses control charts, not audit sampling · Insurance uses mortality tables · Both auditing and sampling are separate applications within business statistics"],
[1,"Which definition describes statistics as 'classified facts respecting conditions of people, stated in numbers'?",["A.L. Bowley","Yule & Kendall","R.A. Fisher","Webster"],3,"Webster defined statistics as 'classified facts respecting conditions of people, stated in numbers.'","Ebook","Other options: Bowley = 'Numerical facts placed in relation to each other' · Yule & Kendall = 'Quantitative data affected by multiplicity of causes' · All three definitions are MCQ staples — author-to-definition matching"],
[1,"Which limitation states that statistical results are 'only approximately true'?",["Statistics deals with groups, not individuals","Statistics results are sample-based and therefore approximate, not exact","Statistics is liable to be misused","Statistics can only be used with numerical data"],1,"Limitation #4: Results are only approximately true — because statistics is often based on samples, not complete enumeration.","Ebook","Other options: Sample-based conclusions = approximations — this is inherent to statistical inference · Misuse = different limitation (#6) · Numerical data only = limitation #1"],
[1,"Planning as an area of statistics involves:",["Setting UCL and LCL for production","Estimating mortality rates","Assessing resources and evaluating growth rate feasibility","Designing questionnaires for surveys"],2,"Statistics in planning involves assessing available resources and evaluating whether projected growth rates are feasible.","Ebook","Other options: UCL/LCL = quality control, not planning · Mortality = insurance application · Questionnaire design = data collection methodology"],
[1,"How many limitations of statistics are typically listed in the standard classification?",["5","6","7","9"],2,"7 limitations of statistics: numerical only, groups not individuals, true on average, approximately true, only one set of methods, liable to misuse, must be used by experts.","Ebook","Other options: 9 is the number of functions/scope, not limitations · 6 is the number of characteristics of statistics · Confusing 7 limitations with 6 characteristics is a very common MCQ trap"],
[1,"Statistics 'deals with groups not individuals'. This means:",["Statistical analysis is always wrong for individual data","A single value is not statistics; it requires an aggregate of related observations","Groups of people are the only valid subject for statistics","Statistics is only applicable to social sciences"],1,"A single figure does not constitute statistics. Statistics requires an aggregate of related facts — one data point tells you nothing.","Ebook","Other options: This is also expressed as characteristic #2: 'Aggregate of facts' · Both the characteristic and limitation sides of this are frequently tested · Statistics can apply to any domain — not just social sciences"],
[1,"The application of statistical methods to practical real-world problems is called:",["Inductive Statistics","Applied Statistics","Inferential Statistics","Descriptive Statistics"],1,"Applied statistics is the branch that applies statistical methods (from theoretical statistics) to real-world practical problems.","Ebook","Other options: Inductive = generalisations from sample to population · Inferential = hypothesis testing about population · Descriptive = organising, summarising, presenting data"],
[2,"The primary purpose of classification in statistics is to:",["Present data in graphical form","Organise raw data into homogeneous groups for analysis","Calculate averages and dispersion","Collect data through questionnaires"],1,"Classification organises a complex, unorganised mass of raw data into homogeneous groups, making it interpretable.","Ebook","Other options: Graphical presentation = tabulation + diagrammatic presentation (Module 4) · Classification is the prerequisite for tabulation, not the same as it · Averages = measures of central tendency (Module 5)"],
[2,"Which characteristic of classification means 'signifies unity in diversity'?",["Data arranged into homogeneous groups","Classification signifies unity in diversity","May be based on measurable or non-measurable characteristics","Basis is similarity of characteristics"],1,"Classification groups diverse items based on their similarities — creating unity within each class despite overall diversity in the dataset.","Ebook","Other options: This is characteristic #3 of classification · Homogeneous grouping is characteristic #1 · The phrase 'unity in diversity' is a direct MCQ target"],
[2,"The requisite 'exhaustive' in a good classification means:",["Each item must be in only one class","Every item in the data must fit into some class — no item can be left out","Classification must be flexible enough to accommodate changes","Classes must be clearly defined"],1,"Exhaustive = every single item in the dataset must fit into one of the classes; no item should be left unclassified.","Ebook","Other options: Each item in ONE class only = Mutually Exclusive (different requisite) · Flexible = adjustable to changed situations (separate requisite) · Exhaustive vs Mutually Exclusive is the most common MCQ confusion"],
[2,"'Mutually exclusive' as a requisite of classification means:",["All items must be classified","Each item can belong to only one class","The classification must remain stable over time","The classification should be relevant to the investigation objective"],1,"Mutually exclusive = each item belongs to ONE and only one class. No overlapping between classes.","Ebook","Other options: All items classified = Exhaustive · Same principle over time = Stable · Relevant to objective = Suitable"],
[2,"The requisite 'stable' in classification means:",["Classes should not change between different studies","The same classification principle is used throughout, enabling comparison over time","Data should remain unchanged after classification","Classification should not be affected by sampling errors"],1,"Stable = the same classification principle is applied throughout the study, enabling meaningful comparison across different time periods.","Ebook","Other options: Stable ≠ inflexible — Flexible is a separate (complementary) requisite · Stable enables comparability over time — important for longitudinal data · Flexible allows adjustment to new situations; Stable ensures consistency"],
[2,"The requisite 'revealing' in a good classification means:",["All items must be disclosed in a public report","Classification must bring out essential features — not too few or too many classes","Hidden patterns in data must always be shown","Classification must be transparent and unambiguous"],1,"Revealing = classification must highlight essential features. Too few classes hide patterns; too many make data unwieldy.","Ebook","Other options: Unambiguous is a separate requisite meaning no confusion in classification · 'Not too few or too many classes' is the key phrase for 'Revealing' · This connects to Sturge's formula in continuous frequency distributions"],
[2,"Classification based on time of occurrence is called:",["Geographical Classification","Qualitative Classification","Chronological Classification","Conditional Classification"],2,"Chronological classification arranges data according to time — e.g., annual national income, monthly expenditure over years.","Ebook","Other options: Geographical = by location/region (state-wise sales) · Qualitative = by non-measurable attribute (sex, religion) · Quantitative = by measurable variable (height, income)"],
[2,"Classification by characteristics such as sex, marital status, or colour is called:",["Quantitative Classification","Chronological Classification","Geographical Classification","Qualitative (Attributes) Classification"],3,"Qualitative or attributes classification groups data based on non-measurable characteristics like sex, religion, marital status.","Ebook","Other options: Quantitative = measurable variables (height, income, age) · Chronological = time-based · Geographical = location-based"],
[2,"A dichotomous classification divides data into:",["More than two categories based on multiple attributes","Two categories based on presence or absence of an attribute","Classes based on geographical regions","Groups based on quantitative measurement"],1,"Dichotomous (twofold) classification = exactly 2 categories, based on presence or absence of an attribute (e.g., Male/Female, Literate/Illiterate).","Ebook","Other options: Manifold = more than 2 categories · Two-way = classified by 2 attributes simultaneously (different concept) · Dichotomous = twofold — remember the prefix 'di' means two"],
[2,"A discrete variable is one that:",["Can take any value within a range","Takes only specific, countable values in an interval","Is always measured in whole numbers only","Cannot be classified into a frequency distribution"],1,"A discrete variable takes only specific (countable) values — e.g., number of children (0, 1, 2, 3...). It cannot take fractional values.","Ebook","Other options: Continuous variable can take any value including decimals (e.g., ₹1,500.25) · Discrete does not mean only whole numbers in all cases — but values are specific and countable · Income is continuous (can be ₹1,500.25); number of rooms is discrete"],
[2,"Sturge's Formula n = 1 + 3.322 × log₁₀N is used to determine:",["The width of class intervals","The number of classes in a frequency distribution","The mid-value of a class","The frequency density of a class"],1,"Sturge's formula determines the optimal number of classes (n) for a continuous frequency distribution, where N = total observations.","Ebook","Other options: Width = (L − S) / n, a different formula · Mid-value = (Lower + Upper limit) / 2 · Frequency density = Frequency / Width of class"],
[2,"In the exclusive method of class intervals, the interval 10–20:",["Includes both 10 and 20","Includes 10 but excludes 20","Excludes 10 but includes 20","Excludes both 10 and 20"],1,"Exclusive method: each class includes its lower limit (≥) but excludes the upper limit (<). The value 20 belongs to the next class (20–30).","Ebook","Other options: Inclusive method includes both limits: ≥ lower AND ≤ upper · Exclusive method upper limit = lower limit of next class — no overlap · Exclusive method requires no adjustment for class boundaries"],
[2,"The inclusive method of class intervals requires the use of:",["Class mid-values","Class boundaries to avoid gaps","Sturge's formula","Frequency density"],1,"Inclusive method (both limits included) creates gaps between classes (e.g., 10–19, 20–29). Class boundaries (9.5, 19.5...) are needed to make it continuous.","Ebook","Other options: Exclusive method needs no class boundaries — upper of one = lower of next · Class boundaries = (upper + lower of adjacent classes) / 2 · Inclusive is used when variable is discrete (e.g., scores out of 100)"],
[2,"A 'less than' cumulative frequency distribution is constructed by:",["Subtracting frequencies from the highest class downward","Adding frequencies progressively from the lowest to the highest class","Dividing each frequency by the total","Calculating frequency density for each class"],1,"Less-than cumulative frequency: add frequencies progressively from the lowest class to the highest — gives 'less than' cumulative totals.","Ebook","Other options: More-than cumulative: add from highest to lowest class · Relative frequency: each frequency divided by total — not cumulative · Less than ogive uses less-than cumulative frequency; More than ogive uses more-than"],
[2,"Frequency density is defined as:",["Frequency multiplied by class width","Frequency divided by class width","Cumulative frequency up to a class","Relative frequency expressed as a percentage"],1,"Frequency density = Frequency / Width of class. It gives the rate of concentration of observations and is used for histogram heights when class widths are unequal.","Ebook","Other options: Frequency × width would give area (used differently in histograms) · Cumulative frequency is a running total, not a density · Frequency density is essential for histograms with unequal class intervals"],
[2,"A bivariate frequency distribution classifies data by:",["One characteristic at a time","Two characteristics simultaneously","More than two characteristics simultaneously","Time and location together"],1,"Bivariate = two characteristics simultaneously. The result is a contingency table.","Ebook","Other options: Univariate = one characteristic only · Multivariate = more than two characteristics · The contingency table is the specific representation for bivariate distributions"],
[2,"A bivariate frequency distribution is represented by a:",["Histogram","Pie chart","Contingency table","Ogive"],2,"A bivariate frequency distribution is represented as a contingency table — rows for one variable, columns for another.","Ebook","Other options: Histogram = single continuous variable frequency distribution · Ogive = cumulative frequency curve · Pie chart = proportional representation of parts of a whole"],
[2,"How many requisites of a good classification are typically listed?",["5","6","7","8"],3,"8 requisites: Unambiguous, Exhaustive, Mutually Exclusive, Flexible, Stable, Suitable, Homogeneous, Revealing.","Ebook","Other options: 6 = characteristics of statistics (Module 1) · 9 = objectives of tabulation (Module 3) · Memorise: UEMFSSHR (Unambiguous, Exhaustive, Mutually exclusive, Flexible, Stable, Suitable, Homogeneous, Revealing)"],
[2,"The mid-value of the class interval 20–30 is:",["20","25","30","10"],1,"Mid-value = (Lower limit + Upper limit) / 2 = (20 + 30) / 2 = 25.","Ebook","Other options: Always (lower + upper) / 2 — straightforward formula · Mid-value is used in arithmetic mean calculation for grouped data · For class 10–20: mid-value = 15; for 30–40: mid-value = 35"],
[2,"Which type of variable can take the value ₹1,500.25?",["Discrete variable","Continuous variable","Attribute variable","Ordinal variable"],1,"Income (₹1,500.25) is a continuous variable — it can take any value including fractions within a range.","Ebook","Other options: Discrete = specific countable values only (like number of children) · Attributes = non-measurable (sex, religion) · Height and age are also continuous variables"],
[2,"In a continuous frequency distribution, the recommended number of classes is between:",["2 and 5","5 and 20","10 and 50","3 and 10"],1,"Minimum 5–6 classes, maximum 20 — too few hides patterns, too many makes the distribution unwieldy.","Ebook","Other options: This links to the 'Revealing' requisite of classification · Sturge's formula gives the precise optimal number · Below 5 classes = patterns obscured; above 20 = defeats purpose of grouping"],
[2,"Classification based on geographical regions is best illustrated by:",["Annual sales data for 10 years","State-wise sales data across India","Gender distribution of employees","Income distribution of households"],1,"Geographical (spatial) classification arranges data by location — e.g., state-wise sales data.","Ebook","Other options: Annual data over time = chronological · Gender = qualitative/attribute classification · Income = quantitative classification"],
[2,"Relative frequency distribution replaces actual frequencies with:",["Cumulative totals","Frequency densities","Proportions or percentages of total observations","Mid-values of class intervals"],2,"Relative frequency = each class frequency expressed as a proportion or percentage of total observations — useful for comparing datasets of different sizes.","Ebook","Other options: Cumulative = running total (less-than or more-than) · Frequency density = freq / class width · Relative frequency enables comparison across datasets with different N"],
[3,"Tuttle defined a statistical table as:",["A graphical representation of numerical data in rows and columns","The logical listing of related quantitative data in vertical columns and horizontal rows with sufficient explanatory words","A systematic arrangement of data into classes and subclasses","A collection of numerical facts arranged chronologically"],1,"Tuttle: 'A statistical table is the logical listing of related quantitative data in vertical columns and horizontal rows, with sufficient explanatory and qualifying words to make clear the full meaning of the data and their origin.'","Ebook","Other options: Graphical representation = diagrammatic presentation (Module 4), not tabulation · Classification = prerequisite for tabulation, different concept · Tuttle's definition emphasises 'explanatory words' — that is the distinguishing phrase"],
[3,"Which is the correct relationship between classification and tabulation?",["Tabulation precedes classification","Classification and tabulation are the same process","Classification is the prerequisite; tabulation follows","Tabulation deals with raw data; classification deals with organised data"],2,"Classification is the first step — it divides data into groups. Tabulation follows — it presents classified data systematically in rows and columns.","Ebook","Other options: The sequence is always: Collect → Classify → Tabulate → Present · This sequence is a direct MCQ target · Tabulation without prior classification produces meaningless tables"],
[3,"Column headings in a statistical table are called:",["Stubs","Captions","Head-notes","Footnotes"],1,"Captions = column headings. Stubs = row headings. This is a commonly reversed MCQ trap.","Ebook","Other options: Stubs are ROW headings — Caption vs Stub is the classic MCQ confusion · Head-note = below the title, indicates unit of measurement · Footnote = below the last line, explains abbreviations or provides extra notes"],
[3,"Row headings in a statistical table are called:",["Captions","Stubs","Head-notes","Table numbers"],1,"Stubs = row headings (left side of table). Captions = column headings (top of table).","Ebook","Other options: Caption = column heading — always at the top · Stub = row heading — always on the left · Mnemonic: 'S-tub = S-ide (rows on the side)'"],
[3,"The head-note in a statistical table is placed:",["At the very top before the table number","Below the title and indicates the unit of measurement","Below the last line of the table as an explanation","In the main body alongside the data"],1,"Head-note is placed below the title and typically indicates the unit of measurement (e.g., 'in ₹ lakhs' or 'in thousands').","Ebook","Other options: Footnote = below the last line (explains abbreviations, provides source info for non-secondary data) · Source = required when data is secondary; placed at the very bottom · Head-note is in brackets below the title"],
[3,"The most important part of a statistical table that contains the actual numerical data is called:",["Title","Head-note","Main Body","Caption"],2,"The Main Body is the most important part — it contains all the actual numerical data, read column by column (top to bottom) and row by row (left to right).","Ebook","Other options: Title = gives scope and nature of the table — important but not the data itself · Caption = column headings, not the data · 'Main Body = most important' is a direct MCQ statement"],
[3,"The footnote in a table is placed:",["Below the title","At the top of the table","Below the last line of the table","In the title row"],2,"Footnote is placed below the last line of the table — it explains abbreviations, provides qualifying notes or supplementary information.","Ebook","Other options: Head-note = below the title (not the footnote) · Source = also at the bottom but specifically for secondary data origin · Footnote vs Head-note position is a frequent exam question"],
[3,"A table that contains data in its original collected form (not derived) is called:",["Special Purpose Table","Derivative Table","Primary (Original) Table","Cross-classified Table"],2,"Primary (Original) table contains data in the original form as collected. Derivative tables contain derived figures like totals, averages, percentages.","Ebook","Other options: Derivative tables contain calculated figures (totals, averages, ratios) · General purpose tables are large reference tables — different classification axis · This is classification by 'nature of presented data'"],
[3,"A table presenting one characteristic only is called a:",["Two-way table","Cross-classified table","Simple table (first-order table)","Complex table"],2,"Simple table (first-order table) = only one characteristic is tabulated. Complex tables have 2–3 characteristics.","Ebook","Other options: Two-way table = 2 characteristics (sub-type of complex table) · Three-way = 3 characteristics · Cross-classified = classified row-wise AND column-wise"],
[3,"A general purpose table (reference table) is characterised by:",["Highlighting a specific relationship between variables","Being small, analytical, and placed in the body of a report","Containing a large data mass with no specific objective; usually in an appendix","Containing only derived figures like ratios and percentages"],2,"General purpose / reference tables: large data mass, no specific analytical objective, usually placed in the appendix of reports for reference.","Ebook","Other options: Special purpose / text tables = small, highlight specific relationships, in report body · Primary table = original data; Derivative = calculated figures — different classification · Appendix placement is a key identifier for reference tables"],
[3,"How many objectives of tabulation are generally listed?",["5","7","9","8"],2,"9 objectives: simplify, highlight characteristics, clarify objective, minimum space, detect errors, facilitate comparison, facilitate reference, identify trends, facilitate analysis.","Ebook","Other options: 8 = parts of a table (different count) · 7 = limitations of statistics (Module 1) · 9 is the number for objectives of tabulation AND functions of statistics — both are 9"],
[3,"Which objective of tabulation refers to the ability to identify errors in data?",["Facilitate comparison","Detect errors and omissions","Present data in minimum space","Facilitate reference"],1,"Objective #5 of tabulation is to detect errors and omissions — a well-structured table makes anomalies visible.","Ebook","Other options: Facilitate comparison is objective #6 · Minimum space is objective #4 · Detecting errors through tabulation is a quality-check function"],
[3,"General rules for tabulation state that horizontal lines should be used:",["Between every row of data","Only at the top, bottom, and under subtotals/totals","Between each caption and stub","For all cells containing decimal values"],1,"Rule for ruling: vertical lines for columns; horizontal lines only at top, bottom, and under totals/subtotals — not between every row.","Ebook","Other options: Excessive lines make the table cluttered and harder to read · Vertical lines separate columns; horizontal lines mark key boundaries · Simplicity is a general rule — overloading with lines violates it"],
[3,"When is mechanical tabulation preferred over manual?",["When data has a small number of characteristics","When the field of investigation is small","When data is large and speed + accuracy are required","When qualitative data is being processed"],2,"Mechanical tabulation (computers) is preferred for large datasets requiring speed and accuracy. Manual tabulation suits small investigations.","Ebook","Other options: Manual = small field, few characteristics · Mechanical = large data, computers, speed, accuracy · This is a simple factual recall question"],
[3,"A cross-classified table is one where:",["Data is classified by one characteristic in rows only","Data is classified both row-wise AND column-wise","Data is derived from another table","Data comes from secondary sources"],1,"Cross-classified table: classified in rows AND columns simultaneously — each category of one variable can appear with any category of another.","Ebook","Other options: Simple table = one characteristic only · Bivariate frequency distribution → contingency table (similar concept from Module 2) · Cross-classification enables analysis of interaction between two variables"],
[3,"The source note in a table is mandatory when:",["Data is collected by direct observation","Data is derived from another table","Data is secondary — obtained from another agency","Data contains decimal values"],2,"Source note is required when data is secondary (collected by another agency). It indicates the origin of the information.","Ebook","Other options: Primary data (collected directly) doesn't need a source note · Source note is placed at the bottom, after the footnote · This is part 8 of the 8 parts of a table"],
[3,"Which general rule states that tables must include totals, ratios, and percentages where possible?",["Simplicity rule","Accuracy rule","Include interpretative figures rule","Self-explanatory rule"],2,"Rule #6: Include interpretative figures (totals, ratios, percentages) where possible — these aid analysis without requiring additional computation by the reader.","Ebook","Other options: Simplicity = not overloaded with details (rule #1) · Accuracy = entries must be correct (rule #7) · Self-explanatory = table must be complete and independently understandable (rule #5)"],
[3,"A table showing profit/loss figures over multiple years with totals and averages is an example of a:",["Simple table","Primary table","Derivative table","Cross-classified table"],2,"Derivative table contains derived/calculated figures — totals, averages, percentages, ratios — not just raw collected data.","Ebook","Other options: Primary = original collected data, no calculations · Simple = one characteristic — but could be either primary or derivative · Special purpose table = by purpose, not by nature of data"],
[3,"How many main parts does a statistical table have?",["5","6","7","8"],3,"8 parts: Table Number, Title, Captions & Stubs, Main Body, Ruling & Spacing, Head-note, Footnote, Source.","Ebook","Other options: 8 parts of a table (not 7 or 6) · 8 requisites of classification (Module 2) — same number, different concept · Memorise: Table Number, Title, Captions, Stubs, Main Body, Head-note, Footnote, Source"],
[3,"The title of a statistical table should be:",["Long and detailed for complete understanding","Concise and unambiguous, stating scope and nature of the table","Same as the head-note","Placed at the bottom for reference"],1,"Title: concise, unambiguous, placed at the top — states what data the table contains (scope) and what type of data (nature).","Ebook","Other options: Long titles violate the simplicity rule · Head-note is below the title and states units — it is different from the title · Table number (identification) is separate from the title"],
[3,"An 'attractive' table is one that:",["Has as many columns and rows as possible","Uses bright colours and large fonts","Is neatly prepared and draws reader attention without being cluttered","Contains only diagrams and no text"],2,"General rule #8: Table should be attractive — neatly prepared to draw reader attention. This means clean layout, proper spacing, not cluttered.","Ebook","Other options: Attractiveness ≠ decorative — it means clear and readable · Accuracy is more important than attractiveness (from diagrammatic rules, Module 4) · Clutter = too many lines, too many columns — violates simplicity"],
[3,"A special purpose table is also known as:",["Reference table","Text / Summary / Analytical table","Primary table","First-order table"],1,"Special purpose table = Text table / Summary table / Analytical table. It focuses on a specific relationship and is placed in the body of a report.","Ebook","Other options: Reference table = General purpose table (large, in appendix) · First-order table = Simple table (one characteristic) · Primary table = original data — different classification axis"],
[4,"How many advantages of diagrammatic presentation are listed?",["4","5","6","7"],2,"6 advantages: attractive/impressive, simplify complex data, give more information, save time/labour, useful for comparisons, universally applicable.","Ebook","Other options: 9 = limitations of diagrams (not advantages) · 7 = general rules for making diagrams · Confusing 6 advantages with 9 limitations is a common MCQ error"],
[4,"Diagrams are said to give a 'lasting impression' because:",["They remain unchanged even after the underlying data changes","They are visually attractive and create a strong visual memory","They are more accurate than tables","They can represent more characteristics than tables"],1,"Advantage #1: Diagrams are attractive and impressive — they create a lasting visual impression, unlike rows of numbers.","Ebook","Other options: Diagrams are NOT more accurate than tables — they have very low precision (limitation #4) · Tables can represent more characteristics; diagrams are limited (limitation #6) · The 'lasting impression' advantage is linked to advertising and newspaper use"],
[4,"Which limitation of diagrams states they 'can portray a limited number of characteristics'?",["Limitation #1 — vague idea only","Limitation #6 — limited to few characteristics","Limitation #9 — false base line risk","Limitation #7 — liable to misuse"],1,"Limitation #6: Diagrams can portray only a limited number of characteristics — unlike tables which can handle many variables.","Ebook","Other options: Vague idea = limitation #1 · Misuse = limitation #7 (can present illusory picture) · False base line = limitation #9 — diagrams starting from non-zero baseline"],
[4,"Diagrams are a supplement to tabulation, NOT an alternative. This refers to which limitation?",["Limitation #1","Limitation #2","Limitation #7","Limitation #9"],1,"Limitation #2: Diagrams are a supplement to tabulation, not an alternative. Tabulation provides precise data; diagrams provide visual impressions.","Ebook","Other options: Limitation #1 = give only vague idea, not suitable for expert analysis · Limitation #7 = liable to misuse (illusory pictures) · Limitation #9 = false base line diagrams must be analysed carefully"],
[4,"The three factors to consider when choosing a diagram type are:",["Cost, time, and accuracy","Nature of data, purpose, and calibre of audience","Sample size, scale, and colour","Title, footnote, and source"],1,"3 factors for diagram choice: (1) Nature of data, (2) Purpose of diagram, (3) Calibre (education level) of audience.","Ebook","Other options: Cost and time are general project considerations, not specific diagram choice factors · Title/footnote/source = rules for making diagrams, not choice factors · All three factors must be considered together — they're interrelated"],
[4,"A sub-divided bar diagram shows:",["Net quantities such as profit above and loss below the X-axis","Parts of a whole in absolute values stacked within each bar","Two or more data sets side-by-side for comparison","Parts as a percentage of total where each bar equals 100"],1,"Sub-divided (Component) bar = shows parts of a whole in absolute values, stacked within a single bar. Each segment = one component.","Ebook","Other options: % Sub-divided = each bar = 100% (proportional, not absolute values) · Multiple/Compound bar = side-by-side comparison of 2+ datasets · Deviation bar = net quantities above/below X-axis (profit/loss)"],
[4,"A histogram is best described as:",["A smooth curve joining the tops of frequency polygon vertices","A bar chart where bars represent class intervals and heights represent frequencies","A diagram showing cumulative frequencies","A pie chart variant for continuous data"],1,"Histogram = frequency distribution diagram. Class intervals on X-axis, adjacent bars (no gaps), heights = frequency (or frequency density for unequal widths).","Ebook","Other options: Frequency polygon = joins mid-points of histogram tops (not the same as histogram) · Frequency curve = smooth version of frequency polygon · Ogive = cumulative frequency curve (less than / more than)"],
[4,"When class intervals in a histogram are unequal, the height of each bar represents:",["The frequency of that class","The frequency density (frequency / class width)","The cumulative frequency up to that class","The relative frequency of that class"],1,"Unequal class widths: height = frequency density (frequency / width). This ensures area (= frequency) remains proportional regardless of class width.","Ebook","Other options: Equal class widths: height can represent frequency directly · Using frequency for unequal widths distorts the visual — wider classes look more frequent · Area of each bar = frequency (fundamental property of histogram)"],
[4,"A frequency polygon is preferred over a histogram when:",["Class intervals are unequal","Comparing two or more frequency distributions simultaneously","The number of observations is very small","Cumulative frequencies need to be shown"],1,"Frequency polygon is better for comparing 2+ distributions simultaneously on the same diagram — multiple lines don't overlap confusingly like multiple histograms would.","Ebook","Other options: Histogram = single distribution; overlapping histograms look cluttered · Cumulative frequencies = Ogive, not frequency polygon · Frequency curve = smooth version of frequency polygon for large datasets"],
[4,"An ogive is used to graphically determine:",["Mode of the distribution","Arithmetic mean of the distribution","Median, quartiles, and percentiles","Standard deviation"],2,"Ogive (cumulative frequency curve) is used to graphically find median, quartiles, deciles, and percentiles.","Ebook","Other options: Median is found from ogive — intersection of less-than and more-than ogives · Mode is found from histogram (highest bar) or by inspection · Mean requires arithmetic calculation — not graphical from ogive"],
[4,"A pie chart divides a circle into sectors. The total degrees in a pie chart is:",["90°","180°","270°","360°"],3,"A circle = 360°. Each sector is proportional to the magnitude of the item (item value / total × 360°).","Ebook","Other options: Each sector angle = (value / total) × 360 · Pie charts are preferred when there are more than 3–4 components · For fewer components, bar diagrams are clearer"],
[4,"A deviation bar diagram is used to show:",["Parts of a whole stacked in a single bar","Net quantities like profit/surplus (above X-axis) and loss/deficit (below X-axis)","Two datasets side-by-side for comparison","Population distribution by age and sex"],1,"Deviation bar = net quantities. Positive values (profit, surplus) shown above X-axis; negative values (loss, deficit) shown below X-axis.","Ebook","Other options: Sub-divided bar = parts of a whole (absolute values) — no below-X-axis portion · Multiple/Compound bar = side-by-side comparison · Pyramid diagram = population by sex and age"],
[4,"Primary data is collected:",["From published reports and other agencies","For the first time by the investigator for the current objective","From existing government records","Through internet searches and databases"],1,"Primary data = collected for the first time specifically for the current investigation. It is purpose-specific and more reliable.","Ebook","Other options: Secondary data = already collected by another agency, used again · Primary data costs more but is more reliable · Secondary data is cheaper but must be verified for suitability, adequacy, and reliability"],
[4,"Which method of primary data collection involves gathering information from a third party or witness?",["Direct Personal Observation","Questionnaire method","Indirect Oral Interview","Schedule method"],2,"Indirect Oral Interview gathers information from 3rd parties or witnesses (not the direct subject) — useful when respondents are reluctant or information is confidential.","Ebook","Other options: Direct Personal Observation = investigator collects directly from source · Questionnaire = filled by respondent · Schedule = same form as questionnaire but filled by the investigator"],
[4,"The key difference between a questionnaire and a schedule is:",["A questionnaire has open questions; a schedule has only closed questions","A questionnaire is filled by the respondent; a schedule is filled by the investigator","A questionnaire is used for primary data; a schedule for secondary data","A schedule is used only for census surveys; questionnaire for sample surveys"],1,"Questionnaire and Schedule use the same form — the difference is WHO fills it. Questionnaire = respondent fills it. Schedule = investigator fills it.","Ebook","Other options: This is the single most tested distinction in Module 4 · Schedule is more accurate (investigator can clarify doubts) but also more expensive · Questionnaire works only with educated respondents; schedule works with illiterate ones too"],
[4,"Which method of data collection is best suited for large areas with illiterate respondents?",["Questionnaire","Direct Personal Observation","Schedule (filled by investigator)","Local Agencies"],2,"Schedule = filled by the investigator — suitable for extensive areas AND illiterate respondents since the investigator asks and records answers.","Ebook","Other options: Questionnaire requires literate respondents · Direct Personal Observation = most accurate but expensive; used for narrow scope · Local agencies = large area but lacks originality of primary data"],
[4,"The method of data collection that is MOST ACCURATE but MOST EXPENSIVE is:",["Indirect Oral Interview","Local Agencies / Correspondents","Direct Personal Observation","Questionnaire"],2,"Direct Personal Observation is the most accurate method (investigator collects directly) but also the most expensive and suitable only for narrow-scope investigations.","Ebook","Other options: Questionnaire = free from investigator bias but has non-response problem · Local agencies = economical but lacks originality · Indirect oral = economical but may lack homogeneity"],
[4,"A cartogram (map diagram) is used to represent:",["Time-series data as a line graph","Frequency distributions as bars","Data relating to geographical areas (population density, rainfall, crop yield)","Parts of a whole as sectors of a circle"],2,"Cartogram = map diagram. Used for geographically distributed data — population density, bank deposits, rainfall, crop yield by region.","Ebook","Other options: Line diagram = time-series or individual items · Histogram = frequency distribution · Pie chart = parts of a whole as sectors"],
[4,"Which rule for making diagrams states that accuracy is more important than attractiveness?",["Rule #2 — Attractive presentation","Rule #3 — Accuracy","Rule #7 — Simplicity","Rule #1 — Title and footnote"],1,"Rule #3: Use proper scales; accuracy is more important than attractiveness. A visually appealing but inaccurate diagram misleads.","Ebook","Other options: Rule #2 = attractive and neatly drawn — but accuracy takes priority · Rule #7 = simplicity — understandable even by a layman · Rule #5 = index (legend) when different shades/colours are used"],
[4,"A broken scale bar diagram is used when:",["One or a few values are extremely high compared to the majority","Data shows seasonal fluctuations","Bars need to be broken into sub-categories","Negative values need to be shown"],0,"Broken scale bar = used when one/few values are extremely high vs majority low — the break in scale avoids very tall bars while keeping smaller bars readable.","Ebook","Other options: Seasonal fluctuations → time series line diagram · Sub-categories = sub-divided or component bar · Negative values = deviation bar (above/below X-axis)"],
[4,"The 3 checks before using secondary data are suitability, adequacy, and:",["Accuracy","Reliability","Completeness","Cost-effectiveness"],1,"3 checks for secondary data: (1) Suitability for current investigation, (2) Adequacy of data, (3) Reliability of data.","Ebook","Other options: Accuracy is implied within reliability but 'reliability' is the correct term used · Completeness and cost are not part of the standard 3-check framework · All three checks must be satisfied before using secondary data"],
[4,"A pictogram represents data using:",["Bars with frequency on Y-axis","Pictures or symbols proportional to the magnitude of data","Sectors of a circle","Dots on a geographical map"],1,"Pictogram = data represented by relevant pictures/symbols — visually attractive but not precise and difficult to draw.","Ebook","Other options: Cartogram = geographical map (dots on map = different concept) · Pie chart = sectors of a circle · Pictogram is attractive but imprecise — a limitation noted for diagrams generally"],
[4,"Which type of question in a questionnaire asks 'How many employees work in your department?'",["Simple alternative (Yes/No)","Multiple choice","Specific information","Open question"],2,"Specific information questions ask for exact facts/numbers (How many? What is your income?). They require precise factual answers.","Ebook","Other options: Simple alternative = Yes/No answers · Multiple choice = select from given options · Open question = respondent answers in own words (subjective)"],
[4,"Pilot testing a questionnaire before actual use is called:",["Validation","Test check","Pre-screening","Stratification"],1,"Test check (pilot testing) = trying the questionnaire on a small group before full-scale use to identify unclear questions or sequencing problems.","Ebook","Other options: Validation is broader; test check is the specific term used in the syllabus · Stratification = sampling method, not a questionnaire rule · Test check is one of the 12 rules for drafting a questionnaire"],
[5,"Murray R. Spiegal defined an average as:",["An attempt to find one single figure to describe the whole of figures","A value which is typical or representative of a set of data","The sum of all observations divided by their number","The value that occurs most frequently in a distribution"],1,"Murray R. Spiegal: 'Average is a value which is typical or representative of a set of data.'","Ebook","Other options: Clark & Sekkade: 'An attempt to find one single figure to describe the whole of figures' · Sum/count = Arithmetic Mean formula, not a definition · Most frequent value = Mode definition"],
[5,"Clark & Sekkade described an average as:",["A value which is typical or representative of a set of data","An attempt to find one single figure to describe the whole of figures","The median of a symmetrical distribution","A central value free from extreme observations"],1,"Clark & Sekkade: 'An average is an attempt to find one single figure to describe the whole of figures.'","Ebook","Other options: Murray R. Spiegal = 'typical or representative value' · Both definitions are MCQ targets — always check which author is asked · Free from extreme observations is a property of mode and median, not a definition"],
[5,"Which is NOT one of the 7 characteristics of a good average?",["Rigidly defined by algebraic formula","Based on all observations","Capable of further algebraic treatment","Must always equal the median of the distribution"],3,"The 7 characteristics do not include 'must equal the median' — that is only true in a perfectly symmetrical distribution.","Ebook","Other options: The 7 characteristics: rigidly defined, easy to compute, easy to understand, based on all observations, algebraic treatment, not unduly affected by extremes, not much affected by sampling fluctuations · Mean = Median = Mode only in symmetric distributions · All 7 characteristics are MCQ targets — especially 'not unduly affected by extreme observations'"],
[5,"Which characteristic of a good average states it should NOT be unduly affected by extreme values?",["Characteristic #3 — easy to understand","Characteristic #4 — based on all observations","Characteristic #6 — not unduly affected by extreme observations","Characteristic #7 — not much affected by sampling fluctuations"],2,"Characteristic #6: a good average should not be unduly affected by extreme observations — this is where Arithmetic Mean fails (its major demerit).","Ebook","Other options: The AM IS affected by extremes — hence it fails this characteristic · Mode and Median are NOT affected by extreme values — they pass this test · This characteristic connects directly to when to use median (skewed data)"],
[5,"The Weighted Arithmetic Mean formula is:",["X̄w = ΣX / n","X̄w = ΣwX / Σw","X̄w = ΣfX / Σf","X̄w = A + Σfd / Σf"],1,"Weighted AM = ΣwX / Σw (sum of weight × value, divided by sum of weights). Used when items have unequal importance.","Ebook","Other options: ΣX/n = simple AM for individual observations · ΣfX/Σf = AM for ungrouped/grouped frequency distribution · A + Σfd/Σf = short-cut method for grouped data using assumed mean"],
[5,"The combined arithmetic mean of two groups is given by:",["X̄ = (X̄₁ + X̄₂) / 2","X̄ = (N₁X̄₁ + N₂X̄₂) / (N₁ + N₂)","X̄ = N₁X̄₁ / N₂X̄₂","X̄ = (X̄₁ × X̄₂)^0.5"],1,"Combined mean = (N₁X̄₁ + N₂X̄₂) / (N₁ + N₂). You weight each group's mean by its size before averaging.","Ebook","Other options: (X̄₁ + X̄₂)/2 gives the simple average of means — only correct if N₁ = N₂ · The geometric mean formula is (X₁ × X₂)^0.5 — not for combined AM · This is property #4 of AM — a very frequent MCQ calculation"],
[5,"The sum of deviations from the arithmetic mean is always:",["Maximum","Minimum","Zero","Equal to the mean"],2,"Property #1 of AM: The sum of deviations from the mean = 0. Positive deviations and negative deviations cancel out exactly.","Ebook","Other options: Sum of SQUARED deviations from mean is minimum (not sum of deviations) · Sum of absolute deviations is minimum from the median (not the mean) · These two properties of mean vs median are classic MCQ confusion points"],
[5,"The sum of squared deviations is minimum when taken from the:",["Mode","Median","Arithmetic Mean","Geometric Mean"],2,"Property #2 of AM (Least Squares Property): The sum of squared deviations is minimum when measured from the arithmetic mean.","Ebook","Other options: Sum of absolute deviations is minimum from the MEDIAN (different property) · This least-squares property is why AM is used in regression analysis · Mode has no such optimum property for deviations"],
[5,"The major demerit of Arithmetic Mean is:",["It cannot be computed for open-end class intervals","It is too affected by extreme values","It is not based on all observations","It cannot be computed algebraically"],1,"Major demerit of AM: Too affected by extreme values. One very large or very small value distorts the mean significantly.","Ebook","Other options: AM IS based on all observations — that is actually a merit · AM IS algebraically treatable — that is a merit · Open-end classes = demerit #5 (not the MAJOR demerit)"],
[5,"Median is defined as the value that:",["Occurs most frequently","Divides the distribution into two equal halves","Is the sum of all values divided by the number of values","Has the maximum frequency density"],1,"Median = positional average that divides the distribution into two equal halves — 50% of observations below, 50% above.","Ebook","Other options: Most frequent = Mode · Sum / count = Arithmetic Mean · Maximum frequency density = Modal class (not mode itself)"],
[5,"For an even number of observations, the median is:",["The (n/2)th value","The (n+1)/2 th value","The mean of the (n/2)th and (n/2 + 1)th values","The most frequently occurring value"],2,"Even n: Median = mean of the (n/2)th and (n/2 + 1)th values. For odd n: Median = (n+1)/2 th value.","Ebook","Other options: (n+1)/2 is for ODD n only · For even n, there is no single middle value — you average the two middle values · This makes the median for even n an estimated value, not an actual observation (a demerit)"],
[5,"The median can be located graphically using:",["Histogram intersection","Frequency polygon vertices","Intersection of less-than and more-than ogives","The highest point of the frequency curve"],2,"Median is found graphically at the intersection of less-than and more-than ogives — drop a perpendicular to the X-axis to get the median value.","Ebook","Other options: Highest point of frequency curve = Mode · Histogram = used to identify modal class, not median · This graphical method is both a merit of median and a Module 3-4-5 crossover question"],
[5,"The sum of absolute deviations is minimum when taken from the:",["Arithmetic Mean","Geometric Mean","Mode","Median"],3,"Property #2 of Median: The sum of absolute deviations is minimum when calculated from the median.","Ebook","Other options: Sum of SQUARED deviations is minimum from the MEAN (not median) · This distinction (absolute vs squared deviations) is the classic exam trap · Mode has no minimum deviation property"],
[5,"The median is described as the BEST average for skewed distributions because:",["It is based on all observations","It is not affected by extreme values — extreme incomes don't shift it","It can be computed for open-end classes and qualitative data","It can be algebraically treated"],1,"Median is best for skewed distributions (e.g., income) because it is not affected by extreme values — a billionaire's income doesn't pull the median up as it pulls the mean.","Ebook","Other options: Based on all observations = merit of MEAN, not median · Algebraic treatment = merit of MEAN · Median IS suitable for open-end classes — that is a separate merit"],
[5,"Croxton & Cowden defined mode as:",["The value at the point around which items tend to be most heavily concentrated","The value that occurs the maximum number of times","A value typical or representative of a set of data","The average of the two most frequent values"],0,"Croxton & Cowden: 'Mode is the value at the point around which items tend to be most heavily concentrated.'","Ebook","Other options: A.M. Tuttle: 'Value with greatest frequency density in its immediate neighbourhood' · 'Occurs maximum number of times' = informal definition, not attributed to a specific author · Mode ≠ average of two most frequent values"],
[5,"A.M. Tuttle defined mode as:",["The value at the point of maximum concentration","The value with greatest frequency density in its immediate neighbourhood","The value around which most items cluster","The midpoint of the modal class"],1,"A.M. Tuttle: 'Mode is the value with greatest frequency density in its immediate neighbourhood.'","Ebook","Other options: Croxton & Cowden = 'most heavily concentrated' (different phrasing) · Both definitions are exam targets — match author to exact phrase · Frequency density concept from Module 2 reappears in mode definition"],
[5,"When a distribution has two values with maximum frequency, it is called:",["Uni-modal","Bi-modal","Multi-modal","Asymmetric"],1,"Bi-modal = two modes (two values with equal maximum frequency). Multi-modal = more than two modes.","Ebook","Other options: Uni-modal = one mode (most common case) · Multi-modal = more than two modes · Asymmetric describes the shape of the distribution, not number of modes"],
[5,"In a symmetrical distribution, which relationship holds?",["Mode < Median < Mean","Mean < Median < Mode","Mean = Median = Mode","Mode = 3 Median − 2 Mean"],2,"In a perfectly symmetrical distribution: Mean = Median = Mode. All three measures coincide at the centre.","Ebook","Other options: Mode < Median < Mean = positively skewed distribution · Mean < Median < Mode = negatively skewed distribution · Mode = 3 Median − 2 Mean is the empirical relationship for moderately skewed distributions"],
[5,"The empirical relationship between mean, median, and mode for a moderately skewed distribution is:",["Mean = Median = Mode","Mode = Median − 2 Mean","Mode = 3 Median − 2 Mean","Median = (Mean + Mode) / 3"],2,"Empirical relationship: Mode = 3 Median − 2 Mean. This holds for moderately (not severely) skewed distributions.","Ebook","Other options: This formula allows you to calculate any one if two are given · It is approximate — valid only for moderate skewness · For perfect symmetry: Mean = Median = Mode (formula still works: Mode = 3M − 2M = M)"],
[5,"For which type of data is the median the ONLY suitable average?",["Normally distributed numerical data","Qualitative data that can be ranked but not measured","Data with equal class intervals","Data with very small sample sizes"],1,"Median is the only suitable average when data is qualitative but rankable (e.g., ranking students as excellent/good/average/poor) — it cannot be assigned a numerical mean.","Ebook","Other options: Mode can also be used for qualitative data but median is the ONLY suitable average when ranking is possible · Mean requires numerical values — cannot be computed for qualitative data · This is both a merit of median and a use case"],
[5,"Mode is NOT suitable when:",["Data is qualitative and rankable","The distribution has open-end classes","Items have unequal importance requiring different weights","There is extreme skewness"],2,"Mode demerit: not suitable when items have unequal importance (no weighted mode concept). For weighted averages, use Weighted AM.","Ebook","Other options: Mode CAN be used for qualitative rankable data — that is a merit · Mode CAN be used with open-end classes — that is a merit · Unequal importance requires weighting — only AM has a weighted version"],
[5,"In a positively skewed distribution, the correct order of measures is:",["Mean = Median = Mode","Mode < Median < Mean","Mean < Median < Mode","Median < Mode < Mean"],1,"Positively skewed (right tail): Mode < Median < Mean. The extreme high values pull the mean rightward.","Ebook","Other options: Negatively skewed (left tail): Mean < Median < Mode · Symmetric: Mean = Median = Mode · A common MCQ: 'Income distribution is positively skewed' → Mode < Median < Mean"],
[5,"Which measure of central tendency is called the 'centre of gravity' of the distribution?",["Median","Mode","Geometric Mean","Arithmetic Mean"],3,"AM = 'centre of gravity' because it balances positive and negative deviations (property: Σ(X − X̄) = 0).","Ebook","Other options: Median = positional average, not the centre of gravity · Mode = most concentrated value · The centre of gravity analogy is a merit of AM and explains why Σ(X − X̄) = 0"]
  ]
};
